<Project>

  <!--
    Directory.Build.targets - build-time enforcement.

    The guardrail script is wired to the API project's build rather than to
    every project's build, so a full solution build runs it exactly once
    instead of eleven times.

    This is a safety net, not the primary mechanism. The primary mechanism is
    the git pre-commit hook installed by scripts/install-hooks.ps1, which
    catches violations before they can be committed at all.

    ========================================================================
    STATUS AS OF P0-07 (2026-08-15): THIS TARGET IS DEAD CODE. IT HAS NEVER
    RUN, AND THAT IS EXPECTED - NOT A DEFECT.
    ========================================================================

    The Condition below requires MSBuildProjectName == 'Merchandising.Api'.
    That project does not exist yet: src/ contains only .gitkeep in Phase 0,
    and there is nothing in the repository for MSBuild to build. Verified,
    not assumed.

    IT ACTIVATES AT P1-01, the task that creates all eleven project shells -
    including src/Merchandising.Api/Merchandising.Api.vbproj - and then runs
    `dotnet build` as one of its acceptance checks. The Condition keys on the
    project NAME, not on which SDK the project uses, so the plain-shell
    Merchandising.Api that P1-01 leaves behind satisfies it just as well as
    the Web SDK version P1-02 converts it into.

    CORRECTED 2026-08-16. This comment previously said P1-02, reasoning that
    P1-02 "hand-authors" the API project. That conflated authoring the API's
    CONTENT with the project existing and being built. P1-01 builds it first,
    so P1-01's `dotnet build` is this target's first real exercise. Until that
    build runs, do not read this file as evidence that guardrails run during a
    build - they do not, because no build happens. Layers L1-L4 in CLAUDE.md
    section 11 are what is actually protecting the repository today.

    If it misfires on that first P1-01 build, that is expected first-run work
    in exactly the way check-no-csharp.ps1 needed its first execution at
    P0-08 - fix it there rather than treating it as a defect in the plan.

    On the interpreter: this deliberately invokes 'powershell' (Windows
    PowerShell 5.1) rather than 'pwsh'. pwsh is roughly 3x faster and is what
    the Claude Code hooks use, but it is not guaranteed present on a machine
    that only has Visual Studio installed. This target runs once per solution
    build, so correctness on an unprepared machine beats ~1.2 seconds.
  -->

  <Target Name="MerchGuardrails"
          BeforeTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Merchandising.Api' AND '$(MerchSkipGuardrails)' != 'true'">

    <PropertyGroup>
      <MerchGuardrailScript>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)scripts\check-no-csharp.ps1</MerchGuardrailScript>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <Exec Command="powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File &quot;$(MerchGuardrailScript)&quot;"
          Condition="Exists('$(MerchGuardrailScript)')"
          ContinueOnError="false" />

  </Target>

</Project>
